data.content:
There's no `.csproj` yet — just a single file. Here's the lightest approach:

**.NET 8+ ships `TimeProvider`** — a built-in abstraction with zero extra packages. You accept it via constructor injection and call `GetUtcNow()` instead of `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`. In production you pass `TimeProvider.System`; in tests you pass `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider` (one small NuGet package for testing only).

**Changes needed:**

```csharp
// Production code — accept TimeProvider, default to System
public class InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider clock)
{
    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => clock.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

```csharp
// Test code
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 30, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 2, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

// You can also advance time mid-test:
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(5));
```

**Why this is the lightest option:**
- `TimeProvider` is in the BCL — no production dependency added
- Only the test project needs `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`
- Existing callers keep working if you add a default: `TimeProvider? clock = null` and use `clock ?? TimeProvider.System`
- No custom interface or wrapper to maintain

